Lab 19 Setup Explainer Using Analogy's 

Key-based login

🐕 The analogy: a password is a secret word you whisper to Mutley at the door. Alan's dodgy mates can stand outside all night shouting guesses. A key pair works
differently. Mutley holds a wax impression of your signet ring (the public key), and only the real ring (the private key) makes a seal that matches. The ring never
leaves your hand, so there's nothing to overhear or guess. Alan carves a fake ring out of a potato, and Mutley eats the potato and the finger holding it.
The technical point: with key authentication, the server sends a challenge that only your private key can sign. The private key never crosses the network,
which defeats password guessing, spraying and credential theft from the wire.


Turn off password login

🐕 Now that there is a signet ring, Mutley stops listening to whispered passwords at the door. Alan rocks up shouting "password123!" and Mutley doesn't even
lift his head. He just points a paw at the sign: RINGS ONLY.


Setting up the ufw firewall

🐕 Mutley gets a gate. Nobody comes in at all except people from your street (your home network) going to door 22 (SSH). Alan tries climbing in a side window, 
the shutter slams down, and he's left hanging there by his trousers.
The technical point: a default-deny inbound firewall blocks every port except the ones you deliberately open, so a service you forgot about isn't reachable from 
outside.

Instead of editing the main sshd_config file I put the settings in a new file starting with 10-. Explain why this is a better?

The main sshd_config belongs to the OS package. When an update ships a new version, edits can be overwritten, or the update stops to ask which version 
to keep. A separate file that's setup is left alone.

Everything that changed is in one small file with a clear name. Anyone checking the server, including myself in six months or an auditor, can see exactly 
what was hardened without comparing a 120-line file against the default.

The first value wins per setting, not per session. SSH reads all the files, and for each setting (such as PasswordAuthentication) it keeps the first value it 
finds and ignores any later ones. That's why 10- beats Ubuntu's 50-cloud-init.conf. Lower numbers are read first, meaning the first value wins.

Mutley Analogy: Mutley's rulebook comes from Head Office, and every few months they post out a fresh copy and thow away the old one, along with any notes Miss Silky 
scribbled in the margins. So she pins her own card to the front of the kennel: "RINGS ONLY. NO ROOT." Mutley reads the card first and follows it, whatever the Head
Office book writes further along in the pages. Alan once tried slipping a "passwords OK" note into the back of the book, but Mutley had already read the front card,
so the note just went soggy in his mouth. The technical point: drop-in config files keep your hardening separate from the vendor's file, so it survives updates, 
is easy to audit, and takes priority when it's read first.

Why it matters at GRC level: this is configuration management in practice. My security baseline is a small, documented, reviewable change instead of an edit buried
in a vendor file. It's the kind of evidence an auditor asks for under "how do you know your servers stay hardened?"

Setting Up SPLUNK On My Old Laptop

The Analogy

Miss Silky is building a control room in the basement. Every CCTV camera in the kingdom (your logs) will feed into one wall of screens. Before the screens go in, she checks that the wiring in the walls
can carry the signal. Last time, Alan the Fool wired a control room with old kettle cord. The screens blew up and his eyebrows went with them, and they've never grown back. Mutley won't guard a room that
hasn't been inspected.

The technical point: Splunk needs a CPU with AVX instructions for its KV store, and it should run under its own low-privilege account, not root. So we check the hardware before we install anything.

Section 1: Pre-flight Checks Before Installing Anything

This laptop is old, and current Splunk has a hard CPU requirement: these CPU families must have support for Advanced Vector Extensions (AVX), Streaming SIMD Extensions 4.2 (SSE4.2), 
and Advanced Encryption Standard: New Instructions (AES-NI). This support is required for App Key Value Store. It's much better to find out now than halfway through the install.

I ran the command  grep -c avx /proc/cpuinfo and it came back 0 

What it does: counts how many lines of the CPU's information mention AVX.

/proc/cpuinfo: is a special file where Linux lists the CPU's details and features, with one block per CPU core

Check: a number above 0 (often 2 or 4, one per core) means AVX is there. It came back 0 so we have a slightly huge problem

A 0 means this laptop's CPU has no AVX, so current Splunk can't run properly on it. It's annoying, but this is exactly why we checked before installing. Alan wires the control room first and loses his
eyebrows. Miss Silky inspects the wiring, finds kettle cord, and changes the plan before anything blows up. "Checked the vendor's hardware requirements before deploying, found a blocker, re-planned" 
also reads really well in a write-up.

What it means: Splunk's KV store needs AVX. On this CPU it would crash with an "illegal instruction" error. Splunk might still half-run, but that's an unsupported setup and not one I'd build your lab on.

The good news: the laptop isn't wasted. It's already hardened, so it can become a Linux log source. A universal forwarder on it would send its SSH and auth logs to Splunk. Forwarders don't run the KV store,
so they don't need AVX. That gives you Windows and Linux logs to hunt through, which looks better in a SOC portfolio than Windows alone.

Installed a New Modem 

Locking down the Ubuntu server's address and harden the router 

Analogy: at the moment, the router is a cloakroom attendant who hands out a random ticket number to each guest. Today the server got ticket 8. If it's off
when the power drops, it could get ticket 23 next time, and then Mutley's guest list (your ufw rules) and the Splunk forwarders all go to the wrong coat hook.
A DHCP reservation staples the server's name to ticket 8 permanently. Alan once lost his coat ticket and was swapped for a coat rack. Nobody noticed for a week
including Alan.

The technical point: a DHCP reservation ties a device's MAC address to a fixed IP, so the server always gets the same address but the router still manages it
centrally.



Setting Up a Guest WIFI With the New Router

My main Wi-Fi is the house itself, with my study, my server and the cameras. The guest network is the verandah. Guests get a comfy chair and the internet,
but the door into my house stays shut and Mutley sleeps across the doorway. Sandy the everyday user sits happily on the verandah scrolling her phone.
Alan, of course, tries to climb through the window into the study and ends up with Mutley wearing his trousers.

The technical point: a guest network is a separate SSID with client isolation, so guest devices get internet access but can't reach devices on your main LAN.
This is perfect security for my wifi. Alan doesn't understand how to use his phone and has a history of downloading dodgy apps. 











